          %p#Idea
          %h2#section_Idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computer+science"} computer science
            %em blockchains
            — originating in concepts of cryptocurrency, with
            %a{:href => "#WeiDai96"} Wei Dai (1996)
            (
            %em>
              %a{:href => "https://en.bitcoin.it/wiki/B-money"} B-money
            ) and
            %a{:href => "#NakamotoBitcoinAPeerToPeerElectronicCashSystem"} Nakamoto (2008)
            (
            %em>
              %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bitcoin"} bitcoin
            ) — refers to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+order"} linearly ordered
            log files shared in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/undirected+graph"} network
            with multiple nodes and without any central authority, which can be added to but are extremely difficult to otherwise modify — as long as a majority of nodes act non-maliciously (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#FaultTolerance"} below
          %p
            Blockchain can be considered as certain data structure – chain of blocks – which are cryptographically “sealed” by hashes which are for each block added to the next block and which is incremented by one of many parties called nodes which follow a commonly accepted algorithm and each contains a copy of the blockchain (full node) or its essential part. Who adds a block, or whose added block is respected by other nodes is a part of the algorithm (blockchain protocol) and is desirable to be byzantine fault tolerant. The securest algorithm in nontrusted environment (public blockchain) is the proof of work algorithm. Regarding that blockchain is a tool to establish noncentralized record verification it is common to use blockchains to carry records of assets, including cryptocurrencies and other token, off chain assets, credential records, verification proofs and so on. These data and transaction steps belong and are authorized by accounts. Modern blockchain protocols assume also that code handling assets in deterministic manner may be part of the account, when the nodes add new blocks they should also execute the code of the contract, within a blockchain virtual machine; such accounts are called
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smart+contract"} smart contract
          %p
            The phrase
            %em distributed ledger
            is sometimes viewed as a synonym. Technically, distributed ledger is the same idea realized in a more general setup where the record may be organized in non-linear structure, for example when the blocks form a DAG (directed acyclic graph) rather than a single chain, with appropriate protocol.
          %p
          %h2#section_Proof_of_work Proof of work
          %p
            The key idea is that of
            = succeed "." do
              %em proof of work
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/list"} list
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [ B_{1}, B_{2}, \ldots ]
            of ‘blocks’. A
            %em block
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is an
            %em unencrypted block
            of content, which could be anything, together with a
            %em cryptographic hash
            of it (‘signature’) with certain properties.
          %p The key points are the following.
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                The cryptographic hash with the required properties is hard to create, where ‘hard’ means requiring significant computational resources and taking time (e.g. something like
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime"} prime
                factorisation). Use of these computational resources to create the hash is referred to as ‘proof of work’.
            %li
              %p
                The hash of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi B
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_{i+1}
                depends on the hash of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi B
                          %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_{i}
            %li
              %p The hash of a block depends exactly on its contents: any change in contents will lead to a significantly different hash.
            %li
              %p It is easy (requires little resources and time) to check that a particular hash has the required properties.
          %p
            Suppose that we have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            nodes in a network. Each node accepts as the current blockchain the longest valid blockchain which it knows of. If a node
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            adds a block
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            to a blockchain
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L = [ B_1, \ldots, B_m ]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo ′
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mi m
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L' = [B_1, \ldots, B_m, B]
            out to all nodes listening to/connected to it. If the longest blockchain
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo ″
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L''
            which a node
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            knows of has length
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L'
            it drops any attempts it is currently making to add a block to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo ″
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L''
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L'
          %p In such a system, it is extremely difficult for any node to tamper with the blockchain. This is because if it wishes to change the content of a particular block, then because of 2. and 3. it must not only re-create the hash of the tampered block, but also the hash of all blocks after it, as well as the hash of a new block. If any honest node creates the hash of just one new block in the meantime and sends it out to the network, the tampering attempt now has still another block to hash. Because of the computational resources required to create even one valid hash, it is thus highly unlikely that tampering will succeed, and thus a tamperer may soon use up great amounts of resources for no gain. If successful adding of blocks is incentivised appropriately, it is thus more sensible to act honestly.
          %p There are various further details, but this is the rough idea.
          %p#FaultTolerance
          %h2#section_Fault_tolerance Fault tolerance
          %p
            The intended functioning of blockchain networks relies on most but not necessarily all actors acting reliably/non-maliciously towards the protocol, a situation known as
            %em Byzantine fault tolerance
            (BFT), see eg.
            %a{:href => "#WangEtAl22"} Wang et al (2022)
          %p
